<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-François Bergier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/2009">2009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-François Bergier was a Swiss economic historian and professor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eth-zurich">ETH Zurich</a>. From 1996 to 2002 he chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/independent-commission-of-experts">Independent Commission of Experts</a>, known after him as the Bergier Commission, which investigated Swiss gold dealings with Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the fate of assets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> victims and the refugee policy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. He also wrote widely on the economic history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>.</p></div>
